  "live_records": {
    "q-164266": {
      "dom_id": "step-q164266",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Given the elliptic paraboloid $z+4=\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $x$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+4 & =\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{4} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{3}+1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{3} & =3 \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $x=1,7.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(1,0,0)$ and $(7,0,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
