    "q-72191": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72191",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given the elliptic paraboloid $z+13=\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $y$-intercepts of the paraboloid, we substitute $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+13 & =\\frac{(0-5)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ 13 & =\\frac{25}{5}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ 13 & =5+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} & =8 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions $y=3,-5.$ \n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-5,0)$ and $(0,3,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
