    "q-72190": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72190",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the vertex of the elliptic paraboloid $z+4=2(x+2)^{2}+5(y-3)^{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The general equation of an elliptic paraboloid oriented along the $z$-axis with vertex at a point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertex, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+4 & =2(x+2)^{2}+5(y-3)^{2} \\\\ z-(-4) & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{1/5} \\\\ z-(-4) & =\\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{1/5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an elliptic paraboloid that has its vertex at $(-2,3,-4).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
