    "q-73862": {
      "dom_id": "step-q73862",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z-3=\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-3 & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4} \\\\ z-3 & =\\frac{(x-(-2))^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(-2,4,3).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
