    "q-164273": {
      "dom_id": "step-q164273",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Find the saddle point of the hyperbolic paraboloid $z+4=\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The general equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is \n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo find the saddle point, we rewrite our equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+4 & =\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{3} \\\\ z-(-4) & =\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbolic paraboloid with a saddle point at $(3,-1,-4).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
